 bet by 600.\n* On Round 2, your opponent performed action: 0.\n* On Round 3, your opponent performed action: -2.\nYour valid actions are: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold)].\n', 'reasoning': '**The hand analysis has begun.**\n\nOkay, this is tricky.  I\'ve got a set of 10s (Three of a Kind!) but the board... the board is dangerous. 10h, 7s, Jh, Ks, 9d.  Sorted, that\'s 7-9-10-J-K.  That\'s a connected board, and those are scary things in Texas Hold \'Em.\n\nLet\'s break it down:  If the opponent has an 8 or a Queen, they have a straight. Any Queen makes a straight. Any 8 also makes a straight.  We\'re looking at a hand where if I call, I *lose*. I\'m also betting my entire stack.\n\nMy opponent has check-called the flop and turn, and now they\'ve gone all-in on the river. That\'s a strong play. It indicates a strong hand - a monster hand. They\'ve likely hit their draw, or they\'re bluffing, but usually not this aggressive. They\'re telling me "I have the nuts".\n\nThe fact that the river card is a 9d completes the straight, is also really important. My opponent\'s betting behavior is extremely polarized - either they\'re really strong or they\'re weak. The board 7, 9, J, K shows no pairs, but the set.\n\nOkay, let\'s look at the numbers.  We started with 20,000 chips.  After the betting, I have 18,400 left. The pot is approximately 3,000. Now, they\'re shoving for 18,400 into a pot of 3,000.  It\'s an overbet! And a big one!  If I call, I must win about 30-40% of the time to break even.\n\nBut let\'s be realistic - my set has very little equity here. If they have an 8 or Queen, I am toast. The risk is immense, as a 9 means that any Queen can complete a straight. With so many Queen\'s and 8\'s, the range of a straight is the most likely.\n\nGiven the board texture, the opponent\'s aggressive all-in, and the strong probability they have the nuts, this means I need to fold. It is my best chance to save chips.\nFolding is the only way to avoid losing it all.\n\nThe call would be a disaster. I must fold.\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: The board is extremely dangerous, showing 7, 9, 10, J, K. This means any single Queen or any single 8 in the opponent's hand completes a straight (9-10-J-Q-K or 7-8-9-10-J). While I hold a Set of 10s, this hand loses to any straight. The opponent's massive All-in on the river, after check-calling previous streets, strongly indicates they were drawing and have now hit their hand.\n\nReason: The probability that the opponent holds a Queen or an 8 is very high given the action and board texture. Since a straight beats my set, and calling requires risking my entire stack, the expected value of calling is negative. Folding preserves my remaining chips and lead.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
